{
  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The destruction of the wells and grain stores violated the prohibition in Additional Protocol I art.54(2) and the corresponding rule in Additional Protocol II art.14.",
      "reasoning": "The wells and grain stores are objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population. The Drennick order expressly aimed to deny these objects to the civilians, which falls squarely within the prohibited purpose described in the treaties. Both Protocol I art.54(2) and Protocol II art.14 forbid attacking, destroying, removing or rendering useless such objects for the purpose of depriving civilians of their sustenance value, irrespective of motive.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "54(2)",
          "quoted_text": "It is prohibited to attack, destroy, remove or render useless objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, such as ... drinking water installations and supplies ... for the specific purpose of denying them for their sustenance value to the civilian population or to the adverse Party, whatever the motive"
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_ii",
          "article": "14",
          "quoted_text": "Starvation of civilians as a method of combat is prohibited. It is therefore prohibited to attack, destroy, remove or render useless, for that purpose, objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, such as ... drinking water installations and supplies ..."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The exception in Additional Protocol I art.54(3) does not apply to the destruction of the wells and grain stores.",
      "reasoning": "Art.54(3) limits the exception to objects used \"as sustenance solely for the members of its armed forces\" or \"in direct support of military action,\" and even then bars actions that would leave the civilian population with inadequate food or water. The wells and grain stores were not used by Drennick forces for their own sustenance nor for any direct military purpose; they were civilian infrastructure. Destroying them would inevitably leave the civilian population without water and food, satisfying the prohibition in the second half of paragraph 3.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "54",
          "quoted_text": "The prohibitions in paragraph 2 shall not apply to such of the objects covered by it as are used by an adverse Party: (a) as sustenance solely for the members of its armed forces; or (b) if not as sustenance, then in direct support of military action, provided, however, that in no event shall actions against these objects be taken which may be expected to leave the civilian population with such inadequate food or water as to cause its starvation or force its movement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The derogation provision in Additional Protocol I art.54(5) cannot justify the destruction because Drennick was not defending its own national territory.",
      "reasoning": "Art.54(5) allows derogation \"where required by imperative military necessity\" only for a Party defending its national territory against invasion. Here Drennick was the occupying power in Calvaran territory, not defending its own soil. Consequently, the condition for derogation is not met, and the prohibition remains fully applicable.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "54(5)",
          "quoted_text": "In recognition of the vital requirements of any Party to the conflict in the defence of its national territory against invasion, derogation from the prohibitions contained in paragraph 2 may be made by a Party to the conflict within such territory under its own control where required by imperative military necessity."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
